Rather than looping, you can vectorize this "calculation" quite easily by using an offset vector. This will be much faster as long as you have the available RAM to hold the vectors in memory.

 x1
      TIME   growth
1  2000-Q1 1.119119
2  2000-Q2 0.699282
3  2000-Q3 0.392123
4  2000-Q4 0.348029
5  2001-Q1 1.123852
6  2001-Q2 0.757552
7  2001-Q3 0.641697
8  2001-Q4 0.344408
9  2002-Q1 0.397346
10 2002-Q2 0.809702
11 2002-Q3 0.952811
12 2002-Q4 0.879049
13 2003-Q1 0.706252
14 2003-Q2 0.910393
15 2003-Q3 0.774214
16 2003-Q4 0.730012
17 2004-Q1 0.676460
> x2
  id surveydate
1  1    2000-Q3
2  2    2000-Q4
3  3    2001-Q1
4  4    2000-Q3
5  5    2000-Q4
6  6    2001-Q1
7  7    2000-Q3
8  8    2000-Q4
9  9    2001-Q1
> x1$growth_minus <- c(NA, x1$growth[1:nrow(x1)-1])
> x1$growth_plus <- c(x1$growth[2:nrow(x1)], NA)
> merge(x1, x2, by.x = "TIME", by.y = "surveydate")
     TIME   growth growth_minus growth_plus id
1 2000-Q3 0.392123     0.699282    0.348029  1
2 2000-Q3 0.392123     0.699282    0.348029  7
3 2000-Q3 0.392123     0.699282    0.348029  4
4 2000-Q4 0.348029     0.392123    1.123852  2
5 2000-Q4 0.348029     0.392123    1.123852  5
6 2000-Q4 0.348029     0.392123    1.123852  8
7 2001-Q1 1.123852     0.348029    0.757552  6
8 2001-Q1 1.123852     0.348029    0.757552  3
9 2001-Q1 1.123852     0.348029    0.757552  9

Matt

